This project implements and evaluates a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)
system using a custom GPT-style language model for open-domain question answer-
ing. We first construct a transformer-based decoder model (GPT-small) equipped
with rotary positional embeddings (RoPE), RMSNorm, and grouped query atten-
tion (GQA), pretraining it on a large corpus and fine-tuning it on summarization
and classification tasks. For the RAG pipeline, we integrate a BM25-based re-
triever with both our custom model and a large instruction-tuned language model
(Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct), enabling dynamic retrieval and augmentation of external
knowledge for each query. We explore advanced prompting strategies, includ-
ing few-shot examples and chain-of-thought reasoning, and investigate context
compression techniques to improve efficiency and relevance. Our results demon-
strate the effectiveness of RAG for leveraging up-to-date information, highlight
the impact of model size and prompting on performance, and provide insights into
the challenges and opportunities for further enhancements in retrieval-augmented
language models